Treasure rule 1, decided 11/20 after the sec.v2 rescan (docs/proposals/coverage-distribution.md)

A sec.v2 record whose strongest finding is conditional (it matters only in some uses, or pickfit could not settle
whether the code reaches it) loses the 79 cap (known_vulnerability) and gets a line on the page instead; blocked and
exposed stay capped, and a sec.v1 check with status vulnerable keeps its own cap. Its security.status still reads
vulnerable, so the MCP server and the page still warn. This changes schema.SECURITY_CAP_LEVELS from WARN_LEVELS to
{"blocked", "exposed"} (and the score.py docstring line), the tests that pinned the conditional cap (reworded, none
removed), and the How page's sentence on the cap with its worker test, so the page says the same as the rule.

Apply on main as it is after the five 11/19 patches and the 11/20 rescan (postlaunch/20261120), then refresh at once:
the stored scores still carry the cap and the records do not validate until `uv run pickfit score --refresh`
rewrites them (offline, no model call). Checked on 11/20 in a copy outside every checkout: the refresh rewrites 305
records; treasures 165 -> 203; records at exactly 79.0 186 -> 5; export_schema.py --check passes (the JSON schema
does not change); the full Python suite passes (2369 passed); the Worker builds its index (1211 entries), typechecks,
and its tests pass but test/cloudrun.test.ts, which needs aws4fetch, missing from that environment's node_modules.

